  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Factor an m x n matrix into singular values and orthogonal left and right singular vectors.\n\nSVD reveals geometric matrix transformations as an initial rotation, axis scaling, and second rotation.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nA = U \\Sigma V^T, \\quad U^T U = I, \\quad V^T V = I\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Compute Singular Value Decomposition via numerical linear algebra routines\n2. Extract singular value diagonal spectrum in descending order\n3. Return orthonormal basis matrices U and V^T\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'singular_values', 'left_singular_vectors', and 'right_singular_vectors'.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: SVD Components\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nLinear algebra is the foundational mathematical language of machine learning. Data samples, image pixels, and word embeddings are all represented as multidimensional vectors and matrices. **SVD Components** transforms coordinate systems, extracts principal axes of variation, or decomposes complex transformations into simpler geometric building blocks.\n\nSVD reveals geometric matrix transformations as an initial rotation, axis scaling, and second rotation. In this problem, the goal is to: factor an m x n matrix into singular values and orthogonal left and right singular vectors.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nA = U \\Sigma V^T, \\quad U^T U = I, \\quad V^T V = I\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Compute Singular Value Decomposition via numerical linear algebra routines\n2. **Phase 2**: Extract singular value diagonal spectrum in descending order\n3. **Phase 3**: Return orthonormal basis matrices U and V^T\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Feature Compression & Projections**: Operations like low-rank approximations and orthogonal projections compress high-dimensional feature spaces while preserving maximum variance.\n* **Numerical Stability**: When inverting matrices or solving linear systems, ill-conditioned matrices (matrices with very small determinants or high condition numbers) require regularization to prevent numerical instability.\n* **Vectorized Execution**: Expressing computations as matrix multiplications allows GPUs and CPUs to leverage hardware tensor cores for maximum throughput.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: matrix = [[3.0, 2.0, 2.0], [2.0, 3.0, -2.0]]\n\nOutput: {\"singular_values\": [5.0, 3.0], \"left_singular_vectors\": [[-0.707107, -0.707107], [-0.707107, 0.707107]], \"right_singular_vectors\": [[-0.707107, -0.707107, -0.0], [-0.235702, 0.235702, -0.942809]]}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated la_svd_components under standard test conditions."
  ],
